GODOT WEBGPU PATCH: don't eagerly re-export HEAP* under -sEXPORT_ALL

Derived against: emsdk / Emscripten 6.0.9
Upstream status:  not reported upstream as of 2026-10-07; no known upstream fix
Tracked in:       webgpu_notes/TASKS.md Task 12 bug #2 (1.5.10)

WHAT IS BROKEN
  Every `dlink_enabled=yes threads=yes` export logs ~11 of these in the first
  ~300ms, one per freshly spawned pthread worker:

      Uncaught TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'buffer')
          at growMemViews (index.js:1:8540)

  Non-fatal -- the engine boots and runs past them -- but each one aborts the
  module's postamble export block partway through, so on worker threads every
  `Module[...]` assignment emitted after the first heap view is silently never
  made: `PThread`, `terminateWorker`, `cleanupThread`, `addRunDependency`,
  `removeRunDependency`, and the rest. Nothing in this fork reads those off
  `Module`, which is the only reason it has never bitten.

WHY IT HAPPENS
  Emscripten creates the memory views in `updateMemoryViews()` and, when they
  are exported, assigns them onto Module right there:

      Module["HEAP8"]=HEAP8=new Int8Array(b); ...   // src/runtime_common.js, maybeExportHeap()

  That function runs on every thread once it has memory, and again after every
  growth, so `Module.HEAP*` is already correct and already kept current. It is
  the single owner of those properties.

  `exportRuntimeSymbols()` knows this. Its `shouldExport()` skips `HEAP*` with
  the comment "HEAP objects are exported separately in updateMemoryViews".

  `exportLibrarySymbols()` -- the `-sEXPORT_ALL=1` path, three functions below
  it in the same file -- never got that exclusion. So with EXPORT_ALL the ten
  views are exported a SECOND time, eagerly, in the postamble. The acorn
  `growableHeap` pass (tools/acorn-optimizer.mjs) then rewrites each one into
  `(growMemViews(), HEAPxx)`, and `growMemViews()` dereferences `wasmMemory`:

      function growMemViews() { if (wasmMemory.buffer != HEAP8.buffer) updateMemoryViews(); }

  On the main thread `wasmMemory` is created in JS before the postamble runs. On
  a freshly spawned pthread worker it arrives later, in the `load` message, so
  the postamble runs with `wasmMemory` still undefined and the first of those
  redundant assignments throws.

  This fork hits it because `platform/web/SCsub` sets `-sEXPORT_ALL=1` for the
  dlink main module. A non-dlink build has no EXPORT_ALL, takes only the
  `exportRuntimeSymbols()` path, and is unaffected -- which is why the symptom
  has always been dlink-specific.

THE FIX
  Apply the same exclusion `exportRuntimeSymbols()` already applies, leaving
  `updateMemoryViews()` as the sole owner of `Module['HEAPxx']`.

DO NOT "FIX" THIS WITH ACCESSORS
  The tempting version -- export the views as `Object.defineProperty` getters so
  they resolve lazily -- is actively harmful, and this is the thing to carry
  forward. `updateMemoryViews()` *assigns* `Module['HEAP8'] = ...` directly. A
  getter-only accessor makes that assignment throw under strict mode and
  silently no-op otherwise, so `Module.HEAP*` would end up permanently wrong
  instead of merely logging. The lazy-getter idea was written up in TASKS.md as
  the preferred fix before `maybeExportHeap` was found; it was wrong, and the
  claim that went with it (that `Module.HEAP*` goes stale after a memory growth)
  was wrong too -- `updateMemoryViews()` refreshes them on every growth.

VERIFIED
  2026-10-07, emsdk 6.0.9, Chrome headless, real export of the user's project
  with `variant/extensions_support=true` + `variant/thread_support=true`, served
  with COOP/COEP.

  Console: 22 errors before (11 of them this TypeError), 0 after. Engine still
  reports `multi-threaded, GDExtension support.` and reaches its own game
  scripts.

  Generated output: the postamble's `Module["HEAPxx"]=(growMemViews(),HEAPxx)`
  block is gone (0 occurrences), `updateMemoryViews()` still carries all ten
  `Module["HEAPxx"]=` assignments, and `Module["PThread"]` now directly follows
  `Module["GOT"]` where the heap block used to sit.

  Runtime, main thread, after startup -- all ten views reachable off Module and
  NOT broken by removing the eager copies:

      HEAP8    Int8Array      len=208404480  detached=false
      HEAPU8   Uint8Array     len=208404480  detached=false
      HEAP32   Int32Array     len=52101120   detached=false
      HEAPU64  BigUint64Array len=26050560   detached=false   (... all ten checked)
      Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(Module,'HEAPU8') -> value property, not an accessor
      Module.PThread -> object,  Module.addRunDependency -> function

  Note the lengths: 208MB is well past the initial allocation, so the heap had
  already grown and `updateMemoryViews()` had refreshed these properties. That is
  the direct disproof of the "Module.HEAP* goes stale after growth" claim that
  originally motivated the accessor idea.

HOW TO TELL WHEN THIS CAN BE DELETED
  Grep upstream's `exportLibrarySymbols()` in `src/modules.mjs`. If it skips the
  heap views on its own -- or if `shouldExport`'s exclusion has been hoisted to
  cover both paths -- this patch is redundant: delete the file and its entry in
  emsdk_patch.py's PATCHES table. The runtime oracle is a real
  `threads=yes dlink_enabled=yes` export: if it loads with no `growMemViews`
  TypeError on an UNPATCHED toolchain, upstream has fixed it.

--- a/src/modules.mjs
+++ b/src/modules.mjs
@@ -609,6 +609,24 @@ function exportLibrarySymbols() {
   assert(MODULARIZE != 'instance');
   const results = ['// Begin JS library exports'];
   for (const ident of librarySymbols) {
+    // GODOT WEBGPU PATCH (eager heap-view export): skip the memory views here.
+    // updateMemoryViews() already assigns Module['HEAPxx'] for every view it creates
+    // (see maybeExportHeap in src/runtime_common.js), and exportRuntimeSymbols()'s own
+    // shouldExport() deliberately skips HEAP* for that reason -- 'HEAP objects are
+    // exported separately in updateMemoryViews'. This loop never got the same
+    // exclusion, so under -sEXPORT_ALL=1 the views are exported a SECOND time,
+    // eagerly, in the postamble. The acorn growableHeap pass rewrites each of those
+    // into `(growMemViews(), HEAPxx)`, and growMemViews() dereferences wasmMemory --
+    // which on a freshly spawned pthread worker only arrives later, in the `load`
+    // message. So the first one throws there, and every Module export emitted after
+    // it is silently skipped (PThread, addRunDependency, ...).
+    //
+    // Skipping them leaves updateMemoryViews() as the single owner of
+    // Module['HEAPxx'], which is what the non-EXPORT_ALL path already does. Do NOT
+    // 'fix' this by exporting accessors instead: updateMemoryViews() assigns these
+    // properties directly, so a getter-only property would make that assignment
+    // throw under strict mode and silently no-op otherwise.
+    if (/^HEAP(?:U?(?:8|16|32|64)|F32|F64|_DATA_VIEW)$/.test(ident)) continue;
     if ((EXPORT_ALL || EXPORTED_FUNCTIONS.has(ident) || extraExports.has(ident)) && !nativeAliases[ident]) {
       results.push(exportSymbol(ident));
     }
